   Per figure, per part, and the panel key by key: measure_brca_real_2026-10-20.log.
   The block is filled to clinical.panel.PANEL_BYTES (5,600,000), so a better block encoding
   carries more genes, not fewer bytes: it frees nothing until that budget is lowered.

   Duplication
   - figure 4 (expression by ER) and its rows each name every patient 4 times: 4,128 barcodes,
     1,032 distinct, in each; gzip takes the repeats within each part.
   - the plotly template sits in all 10 figures, 7,651 B each, beside ctx-templates.
   - the panel's gene symbols (21,501 B) are repeated in its screen rows.
   - the screen IS the panel: 2,332 screen rows, every one a panel gene. M10.G's
     "one bit per patient per gene" (2,332 x 1,088 / 8 = 317,152 B, not the 0.7 MB TODO.md
     estimated) is the median split of values the block already carries: M10.G needs its JS
     and no new data. Same on the other cohorts: 4,839 to 5,110 screen genes, all panel genes.

2. The fix: ModulePage.pack_figures (M10 sets it). Each figure's three newPlot arguments and its
   rows travel as raw DEFLATE in base64 (figures.pack_json), as written, and the figure's own
   inline script draws them with ctxUnpack (templates/_unpack.js.j2, a synchronous RFC 1951
   inflate after zlib's puff.c, 5,753 B), where it drew them raw before; so every later script
   finds each plot drawn as before. Lossless: the inflated text is the raw text byte for byte.
   The existing Group.packed (draw when reached) was not used: M10's ER and expression groups
   sit above the survival figure, and a linked list scrolls to it (PK), so groups drawing late
   above it would move it.

